A split leaves its halves roots rather than below the Untyped it split,
so the Untyped is free at once and makes a pool over them,
and a revoke below it takes nothing back.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work test qemu-replay
Report: memory that may hold kernel objects overlaps a node not above or below it

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -249,8 +249,8 @@ static int op_untyped(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
         /* Both slots are free, so neither store fails and the split is whole or not at all. */
         struct cap lower = cap_to_untyped(base, half, cap->rights);
         struct cap upper = cap_to_untyped(base + half, half, cap->rights);
-        cap_store(table, arg[1], &lower, u);
-        return cap_store(table, arg[2], &upper, u);
+        cap_store(table, arg[1], &lower, NULL);
+        return cap_store(table, arg[2], &upper, NULL);
     }
     default:
         return KERR_WRONG_TYPE;
